      title: "The Troubles of the 1780s",
      body: `## A postwar depression

Peace brought hard times. British merchants, eager to recover their American markets, flooded the United States with goods on credit. Americans, starved of imports during the war, bought eagerly, and gold and silver drained out of the country to pay for them. Meanwhile, Britain closed its West Indian islands to American ships, cutting off one of the most important prewar markets for American farm products, fish, and lumber. American exports fell; prices collapsed; merchants failed. From about 1784 to 1787, much of the country suffered a severe depression.

## Debts

Everyone was in debt. The United States owed tens of millions of dollars to foreign lenders, especially France and the Netherlands, and to its own citizens, who held certificates issued for loans, supplies, and soldiers' pay. The states had their own large war debts. And individuals owed money to merchants, who owed money to British creditors.

Congress could not pay. Its requisitions to the states went largely unpaid: in the mid-1780s, it received only a small fraction of what it requested, barely enough to pay the interest on the foreign debt. Twice Congress asked the states to amend the Articles to allow it to collect a modest **impost**, a 5 percent duty on imports. Twelve states agreed in 1781, but Rhode Island refused, and amendments required unanimity. A second attempt failed in 1786 when New York would not accept the terms. Congress, as one member complained, could declare everything but do nothing.

## Weakness abroad

The Confederation's weakness was felt abroad. Britain refused to evacuate its forts on the Great Lakes, in violation of the peace treaty, citing the states' failure to pay British creditors and restore loyalist property. Britain also refused a commercial treaty, since Congress could not retaliate against British trade restrictions; each state set its own trade policy, and if one state taxed British goods, the British simply landed them in another. Spain, which controlled New Orleans, closed the mouth of the Mississippi River to American shipping in 1784, cutting off western farmers from their only practical route to market. When John Jay, Congress's secretary for foreign affairs, negotiated a treaty with Spain's envoy Diego de Gardoqui in 1786 that would have given up American navigation rights on the Mississippi for twenty-five years in exchange for commercial privileges, southern and western states were furious and blocked it. Some westerners talked of seceding and making their own arrangements with Spain. In the Mediterranean, Barbary corsairs from North Africa seized American merchant ships and enslaved their crews, and Congress had neither the navy to fight them nor the money to pay the tribute they demanded.

## The states and the debtors

Within the states, the depression produced fierce conflicts between creditors and debtors. Many state legislatures, dominated by farmers and responsive to their voters, passed laws to relieve debtors: issuing paper money that could be used to pay debts, postponing the collection of debts, or allowing debts to be paid in goods rather than money. Rhode Island's legislature issued large amounts of paper money and penalized creditors who refused to accept it.

To creditors—merchants, lenders, holders of government securities—these laws were an assault on property and contracts. And to men like **James Madison**, they were evidence of something deeper. In the spring of 1787, Madison wrote a memorandum titled "Vices of the Political System of the United States." Its list of vices included the failure of the states to comply with requisitions, their encroachments on federal authority and on one another, and—most important—the "multiplicity," "mutability," and "injustice" of state laws. The greatest danger to liberty in America, Madison concluded, came not from a distant tyrant but from majorities in the states, which used their power to violate the rights of minorities, especially property owners and religious dissenters. The Revolution had placed sovereignty in the people; now the question was how to protect the people from themselves.

## Fear of disunion

Some leaders began to wonder whether the union would survive at all. There was talk of dividing it into two or three regional confederacies—New England, the middle states, the South—each with interests of its own. The quarrel over the Jay–Gardoqui negotiations had pitted North against South. If the union broke apart, nationalists feared, the separate confederacies would become rivals, perhaps enemies, open to the intrigues of European powers, just as the states of Europe were. The Revolution's achievement could be lost. Washington, in a circular letter to the states in 1783, had already warned that the fate of the union would decide whether the Revolution was "a blessing or a curse," not only for Americans but for "unborn millions." By 1786 many of his correspondents feared the curse, and some of them said so openly.

## A critical period?

In 1888 the historian John Fiske called the 1780s "the critical period of American history," a time when the nation nearly collapsed and was saved only by the Constitution. That view prevailed for decades. In the twentieth century, historians such as Merrill Jensen challenged it, arguing that the Confederation was working better than its critics claimed, that the economy was recovering by 1787, and that the crisis was largely invented by a nationalist elite who wanted a stronger government for their own purposes. Most historians today take a middle position. The problems of the 1780s were real, especially the government's inability to pay its debts, defend its interests abroad, or restrain the states. But whether they amounted to a crisis depended on where one stood. For creditors and nationalists, they did. For indebted farmers, the real crisis was the tax collector.

> **Pause and consider.** Madison concluded that the greatest threat to liberty came from majorities in the states. How does this differ from the threat the revolutionaries had feared in 1765?`,
